P18-V01 ClientConfig reverse-surface slice (2026-10-15): compared Rust's
ClientConfig definition and consumer call sites with pinned Java 5.5.1
ClientConfig at efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9. Java exposes its 33
setting values through methods; Rust had exposed the backing fields publicly,
including namesrvAddr/namespace storage whose direct writes bypass Java setter
cache behavior. Made all 33 fields crate-private, retained mapped accessors,
added an external compile-fail rustdoc guard, and migrated Core/Producer/
Consumer cross-crate code and integration tests to accessors. Consumer running
information retains Java MixAll.object2Properties field semantics through a
read-only, doc-hidden bridge; raw NameServer/namespace values and null omission
remain unchanged. The first Core config differential run exposed that calling
setNamespace in its fixture builder marks namespaceInitialized true; updated
the builder to construct Java's cloneClientConfig state for uninitialized but
non-null namespace cases. The 21 Core config tests then passed.

Scoped validation on this slice: `cargo check --workspace --all-targets
--locked` passed; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core --test config --locked`
passed (21); `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core --doc --locked` passed (13);
`cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-features --locked` passed (219
unit tests plus all integration/doc targets); and
`cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-producer --all-features --locked` passed (142
unit tests plus all integration/doc targets). Full current-revision P18-V02
gates remain required after this implementation change. No Java reference
files or plan files were modified.

